WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:12.083 --> 00:00:15.792
[This talk is delivered in Spanish
with consecutive English translation]

00:00:16.958 --> 00:00:20.693
(Rayma Suprani: Speaks Spanish)

00:00:20.947 --> 00:00:22.828
Cloe Shasha: When I was a little girl,

00:00:22.858 --> 00:00:25.226
I used to draw
on all the walls of my house.

00:00:25.250 --> 00:00:28.167
(RS: Speaks Spanish)

00:00:33.583 --> 00:00:36.309
CS: Until one day, my mother
got angry and told me,

00:00:36.333 --> 00:00:38.101
"You can only draw on one wall.

00:00:38.125 --> 00:00:39.643
Don't draw on any others."

00:00:39.667 --> 00:00:41.101
(Laughter)

00:00:41.125 --> 00:00:44.601
(Speaks Spanish)

00:00:44.625 --> 00:00:48.476
That was the first time I experienced
an act of totalitarian censorship.

00:00:48.500 --> 00:00:51.518
(Laughter)

00:00:51.542 --> 00:00:53.500
(Speaks Spanish)

00:00:56.708 --> 00:01:00.292
But a lack of freedom
stimulates creativity and empowers it.

00:01:00.875 --> 00:01:04.000
(Speaks Spanish)

00:01:19.042 --> 00:01:21.393
Many years have passed since my childhood,

00:01:21.417 --> 00:01:23.018
but throughout my formal studies,

00:01:23.042 --> 00:01:25.559
I found myself drawing
in the margins of my notebooks

00:01:25.583 --> 00:01:28.226
instead of paying attention
to my professors.

00:01:28.250 --> 00:01:30.018
I studied journalism in college

00:01:30.042 --> 00:01:33.643
with the intention of expanding
my communication and writing skills,

00:01:33.667 --> 00:01:36.268
but the only thing
that I felt connected to in life

00:01:36.292 --> 00:01:37.684
was drawing.

00:01:37.708 --> 00:01:40.500
(Speaks Spanish)

00:01:43.625 --> 00:01:45.434
I was born in a democracy,

00:01:45.458 --> 00:01:48.917
in a country called Venezuela,
which is now a dictatorship.

00:01:49.458 --> 00:01:52.708
(Speaks Spanish)

00:02:01.625 --> 00:02:02.893
For 19 years,

00:02:02.917 --> 00:02:05.268
I was the daily cartoonist
for "El Universal,"

00:02:05.292 --> 00:02:07.809
one of the biggest newspapers 
in Venezuela.

00:02:07.833 --> 00:02:12.726
I really enjoyed translating political
and cultural current events into drawings.

00:02:12.750 --> 00:02:15.500
(Speaks Spanish)

00:02:25.292 --> 00:02:26.643
In the year 2014,

00:02:26.667 --> 00:02:30.268
I got fired from my job at the newspaper
over a cartoon that I drew

00:02:30.292 --> 00:02:33.059
alluding to the health care
system in Venezuela.

00:02:33.083 --> 00:02:35.518
I drew a flat line of a heartbeat monitor,

00:02:35.542 --> 00:02:37.809
but I intentionally drew
the heartbeat line

00:02:37.833 --> 00:02:40.559
in a way that resembled
the signature of Hugo Chavez,

00:02:40.583 --> 00:02:42.518
the former president of Venezuela.

00:02:42.542 --> 00:02:43.559
(Laughter)

00:02:43.583 --> 00:02:46.125
(Speaks Spanish)

00:02:51.833 --> 00:02:54.226
All of this happened
after the newspaper was bought

00:02:54.250 --> 00:02:55.809
by an unknown company,

00:02:55.833 --> 00:02:57.226
and some of us suspect

00:02:57.250 --> 00:03:00.583
that it was the Venezuelan government
who was behind that deal.

00:03:00.958 --> 00:03:04.750
(Speaks Spanish)

00:03:11.833 --> 00:03:14.559
My work as a cartoonist
became more and more of a nuisance

00:03:14.583 --> 00:03:15.934
for the dictatorship.

00:03:15.958 --> 00:03:18.684
They have no tolerance
for any freedom of expression

00:03:18.708 --> 00:03:20.125
or free thinking.

00:03:20.833 --> 00:03:24.458
(Speaks Spanish)

00:03:37.625 --> 00:03:38.893
After I got fired,

00:03:38.917 --> 00:03:41.559
I started to feel unsafe
in my own country.

00:03:41.583 --> 00:03:44.476
I received anonymous calls
and death threats.

00:03:44.500 --> 00:03:47.393
I was mocked publicly
on national television.

00:03:47.417 --> 00:03:49.726
I was eventually forced out of Venezuela,

00:03:49.750 --> 00:03:51.726
and I now live in Miami, Florida,

00:03:51.750 --> 00:03:54.393
where I am free to be
my own editor for my work.

00:03:54.417 --> 00:03:58.042
(Speaks Spanish)

00:04:07.375 --> 00:04:10.809
A political cartoon is a barometer
of freedom in a country.

00:04:10.833 --> 00:04:12.976
That's why dictators hate cartoonists

00:04:13.000 --> 00:04:16.101
and try to eradicate everything
that involves humor

00:04:16.125 --> 00:04:18.833
as a mirror for social
and political issues.

00:04:19.792 --> 00:04:23.292
(Speaks Spanish)

00:04:26.792 --> 00:04:30.351
A cartoon involves a delicate balance
of ideas and drawings

00:04:30.375 --> 00:04:32.434
that reveal a hidden truth.

00:04:32.458 --> 00:04:37.184
(Speaks Spanish)

00:04:37.208 --> 00:04:40.934
And a good cartoon is one that conveys
a plot of a full-length movie

00:04:40.958 --> 00:04:42.476
in a single frame.

00:04:42.500 --> 00:04:45.875
(Speaks Spanish)

00:04:56.417 --> 00:05:00.184
A cartoon needs to communicate
the core of a story with its precision.

00:05:00.208 --> 00:05:01.476
And when it succeeds,

00:05:01.500 --> 00:05:04.643
its message can have the effect
of inoculating people

00:05:04.667 --> 00:05:06.542
with a dose of skepticism.

00:05:06.958 --> 00:05:10.000
(Speaks Spanish)

00:05:22.708 --> 00:05:26.143
Cartoons are drawn
from observation and analysis.

00:05:26.167 --> 00:05:28.476
They are inspired by muses of mythology,

00:05:28.500 --> 00:05:32.268
as well as classical, modern
and paleolithic tales.

00:05:32.292 --> 00:05:33.976
(Laughter)

00:05:34.000 --> 00:05:38.393
When we are told that a modern-day emperor
is wearing new clothes,

00:05:38.417 --> 00:05:42.309
cartoons reveal that the emperor
just might still be naked.

00:05:42.333 --> 00:05:45.792
(Speaks Spanish)

00:05:58.208 --> 00:05:59.684
At one point in my career,

00:05:59.708 --> 00:06:03.518
I drew pigs and compared them
with politicians and national guards

00:06:03.542 --> 00:06:07.559
who were responsible for stopping
peaceful student demonstrations.

00:06:07.583 --> 00:06:09.726
One day, when I got back to my office,

00:06:09.750 --> 00:06:11.601
I had a letter on my desk.

00:06:11.625 --> 00:06:15.976
(Speaks Spanish)

00:06:16.000 --> 00:06:18.976
The letter was from
the Venezuelan Swine Federation.

00:06:19.000 --> 00:06:20.434
(Laughter)

00:06:20.458 --> 00:06:23.792
(Speaks Spanish)

00:06:42.000 --> 00:06:43.268
The letter said,

00:06:43.292 --> 00:06:46.684
"Please do not compare an animal
as wonderful as a pig with politicians.

00:06:46.708 --> 00:06:47.726
(Laughter)

00:06:47.750 --> 00:06:49.351
Pigs are very friendly and noble,

00:06:49.375 --> 00:06:50.851
they can be a great mascot,

00:06:50.875 --> 00:06:52.184
they make good pets

00:06:52.208 --> 00:06:56.018
and they provide sustenance to us
in the form of pork."

00:06:56.042 --> 00:06:57.792
(Speaks Spanish)

00:07:01.958 --> 00:07:03.684
I think they were absolutely right.

00:07:03.708 --> 00:07:05.101
I didn't draw any more pigs,

00:07:05.125 --> 00:07:07.184
but I did keep drawing politicians.

00:07:07.208 --> 00:07:08.226
(Laughter)

00:07:08.250 --> 00:07:10.583
(Speaks Spanish)

00:07:26.458 --> 00:07:29.309
A cartoon travels
on an information highway,

00:07:29.333 --> 00:07:31.393
which seems like it has multiple lanes,

00:07:31.417 --> 00:07:33.393
but in reality, all of these lanes

00:07:33.417 --> 00:07:37.393
lead to a binary response
of either positivity or rage.

00:07:37.417 --> 00:07:39.976
"I like it" or "I don't like it."

00:07:40.000 --> 00:07:41.351
Those are the only responses

00:07:41.375 --> 00:07:44.476
that govern democratic thinking
on the internet.

00:07:44.500 --> 00:07:47.750
(Speaks Spanish)

00:07:55.542 --> 00:07:58.059
We have lost the space for nuanced debate,

00:07:58.083 --> 00:07:59.351
we have no time,

00:07:59.375 --> 00:08:01.934
so we simply respond
with approval or disdain,

00:08:01.958 --> 00:08:04.559
and we let algorithms take over.

00:08:04.583 --> 00:08:07.667
(Speaks Spanish)

00:08:20.750 --> 00:08:24.934
But a cartoon is born
from a space of deep, nuanced thinking.

00:08:24.958 --> 00:08:27.726
Creating a good cartoon
involves repeated failures,

00:08:27.750 --> 00:08:29.643
draft after draft.

00:08:29.667 --> 00:08:32.018
And a cartoonist must shed
their own taboos

00:08:32.042 --> 00:08:35.226
to spark a conversation,
rather than a confrontation,

00:08:35.250 --> 00:08:36.893
through their work.

00:08:36.917 --> 00:08:40.667
(Speaks Spanish)

00:08:54.833 --> 00:08:58.143
In the year 2013, President Chavez died,

00:08:58.167 --> 00:09:01.143
and I had to think about what to draw
as the cartoon of the day

00:09:01.167 --> 00:09:02.434
at "El Universal."

00:09:02.458 --> 00:09:04.684
I was personally happy
that he had passed away,

00:09:04.708 --> 00:09:06.708
because I thought
that the end of his power

00:09:06.732 --> 00:09:10.476
would potentially bring our country
closer to freedom and better times.

00:09:10.500 --> 00:09:14.083
(Speaks Spanish)

00:09:20.000 --> 00:09:23.393
But there were many other people
who were mourning the death of Chavez,

00:09:23.417 --> 00:09:25.560
so there was a divided
sentiment in Venezuela.

00:09:25.584 --> 00:09:26.851
Some were celebrating,

00:09:26.875 --> 00:09:30.018
and others were crying
at the loss of their leader.

00:09:30.042 --> 00:09:33.458
(Speaks Spanish)

00:09:45.250 --> 00:09:46.518
I felt stuck,

00:09:46.542 --> 00:09:49.268
I really didn't know what to draw
in this historic moment.

00:09:49.292 --> 00:09:52.601
And I knew that I couldn't allow
my happiness to seep into my work,

00:09:52.625 --> 00:09:55.893
that I should take the higher road
and respect people's grief.

00:09:55.917 --> 00:09:57.809
So what could I draw?

00:09:57.833 --> 00:10:01.208
(Speaks Spanish)

00:10:15.000 --> 00:10:18.434
I spent many hours drafting
and throwing out papers.

00:10:18.458 --> 00:10:19.893
My editor called me and said

00:10:19.917 --> 00:10:22.143
everything was late
for that day's newspaper

00:10:22.167 --> 00:10:23.934
and asked me when I'd be done.

00:10:23.958 --> 00:10:26.768
It was in the middle of the night
that the idea came to me.

00:10:26.792 --> 00:10:28.476
And we then published a cartoon

00:10:28.500 --> 00:10:31.643
that represented
a historical moment in time.

00:10:31.667 --> 00:10:35.059
(Speaks Spanish)

00:10:35.083 --> 00:10:37.958
A fallen king chess piece in red.

00:10:38.708 --> 00:10:41.542
(Speaks Spanish)

00:10:50.958 --> 00:10:53.101
A good cartoon has a lot of power.

00:10:53.125 --> 00:10:55.643
It can generate action and reaction.

00:10:55.667 --> 00:10:59.268
That's why a cartoonist must
exercise their power responsibly,

00:10:59.292 --> 00:11:00.768
showcasing the truth

00:11:00.792 --> 00:11:03.643
and drawing without
the fear of consequences.

00:11:03.667 --> 00:11:08.059
(Speaks Spanish)

00:11:08.083 --> 00:11:09.726
Having an opinion has a cost,

00:11:09.750 --> 00:11:12.768
and in some countries, that cost is high.

00:11:12.792 --> 00:11:15.583
(Speaks Spanish)

00:11:29.708 --> 00:11:30.976
In Venezuela,

00:11:31.000 --> 00:11:33.976
many young people have been killed
for protesting peacefully.

00:11:34.000 --> 00:11:36.351
There are men and women
who are stuck behind bars

00:11:36.375 --> 00:11:38.101
as political prisoners.

00:11:38.125 --> 00:11:39.393
So over the years,

00:11:39.417 --> 00:11:41.371
I've drawn the faces of imprisoned women,

00:11:41.375 --> 00:11:45.059
because I don't want them
to be forgotten by the community.

00:11:45.083 --> 00:11:47.708
(Speaks Spanish)

00:12:06.792 --> 00:12:09.518
This year at an event
called El Foro Penal,

00:12:09.542 --> 00:12:11.018
which is a criminal forum

00:12:11.042 --> 00:12:13.018
where a group of lawyers do pro bono work

00:12:13.042 --> 00:12:15.018
for Venezuelan political prisoners,

00:12:15.042 --> 00:12:17.601
a young woman approached me and she said,

00:12:17.625 --> 00:12:18.976
"I was imprisoned,

00:12:19.000 --> 00:12:21.726
and then you drew my face and my story.

00:12:21.750 --> 00:12:24.893
It's because of that drawing
that people knew who I was.

00:12:24.917 --> 00:12:27.809
Your cartoon helped me survive
my days in prison.

00:12:27.833 --> 00:12:29.101
Thank you."

00:12:29.125 --> 00:12:32.208
(Speaks Spanish)

00:12:36.125 --> 00:12:38.101
This was a moment that meant a lot to me,

00:12:38.125 --> 00:12:40.059
because I had found a way to collaborate

00:12:40.083 --> 00:12:42.476
with the memories
of my country and its people.

00:12:42.500 --> 00:12:44.875
(Speaks Spanish)

00:13:01.708 --> 00:13:05.434
Last year, I started making
drawings about immigration.

00:13:05.458 --> 00:13:08.309
I drew my own world, my fears,

00:13:08.333 --> 00:13:10.434
my suitcase, my roots

00:13:10.458 --> 00:13:13.601
and everything that I had
to leave behind in Venezuela.

00:13:13.625 --> 00:13:16.809
I also drew my joy
in the face of this new opportunity

00:13:16.833 --> 00:13:18.934
as an immigrant in the United States.

00:13:18.958 --> 00:13:22.333
(Speaks Spanish)

00:13:25.667 --> 00:13:27.768
From there, I worked
on a series of drawings

00:13:27.792 --> 00:13:31.351
that represented the experience
and psychology of immigration.

00:13:31.375 --> 00:13:34.083
(Speaks Spanish)

00:13:45.000 --> 00:13:48.101
Being an immigrant
is like moving to another planet.

00:13:48.125 --> 00:13:51.643
At first, you don't understand
anything about your new world.

00:13:51.667 --> 00:13:53.893
There are new codes, a new language

00:13:53.917 --> 00:13:56.726
and unfamiliar tools
you need to learn how to use

00:13:56.750 --> 00:13:59.309
in order to adapt to your new life.

00:13:59.333 --> 00:14:01.875
(Speaks Spanish)

00:14:04.833 --> 00:14:07.893
Being an immigrant is the closest thing
to being an astronaut

00:14:07.917 --> 00:14:09.601
who landed on the moon.

00:14:09.625 --> 00:14:11.875
(Speaks Spanish)

00:14:25.542 --> 00:14:28.934
Over time, that series of drawings
became a traveling exhibition,

00:14:28.958 --> 00:14:30.809
called "I, Immigrant."

00:14:30.833 --> 00:14:33.101
And the exhibition traveled
to multiple cities,

00:14:33.125 --> 00:14:36.309
including Miami, Houston,
Madrid, Barcelona,

00:14:36.333 --> 00:14:38.434
and we're hoping for more places.

00:14:38.458 --> 00:14:41.250
(Speaks Spanish)

00:14:46.458 --> 00:14:49.434
The show has become
a meeting space for the diaspora,

00:14:49.458 --> 00:14:53.351
for people to gather and recognize
their shared experiences of suffering

00:14:53.375 --> 00:14:55.184
that come with immigration.

00:14:55.208 --> 00:14:57.792
(Speaks Spanish)

00:15:15.000 --> 00:15:16.809
What I want these drawings to convey

00:15:16.833 --> 00:15:19.268
is that an immigrant is not a criminal.

00:15:19.292 --> 00:15:22.184
An immigrant is a person
whose life has been broken.

00:15:22.208 --> 00:15:25.268
A person who has very likely
been separated from their family

00:15:25.292 --> 00:15:27.184
under inhumane conditions.

00:15:27.208 --> 00:15:30.833
Who has been forced to leave their country
in search of a better life.

00:15:31.333 --> 00:15:34.333
(Speaks Spanish)

00:15:43.708 --> 00:15:45.851
A drawing can be a synthesis of a place,

00:15:45.875 --> 00:15:49.018
a universe, a country or a society.

00:15:49.042 --> 00:15:52.226
It can also represent
the inner workings of someone's mind.

00:15:52.250 --> 00:15:56.309
For me, drawing cartoons
is a form of resistance.

00:15:56.333 --> 00:15:58.833
(Speaks Spanish)

00:16:09.625 --> 00:16:12.059
A cartoon is like a Rosetta stone.

00:16:12.083 --> 00:16:13.768
If we throw it into outer space,

00:16:13.792 --> 00:16:16.309
a future alien would be
able to know with certainty

00:16:16.333 --> 00:16:17.601
that in the past,

00:16:17.625 --> 00:16:20.893
we once had a civilized world
with free thinking.

00:16:20.917 --> 00:16:23.875
(Speaks Spanish)

00:16:27.542 --> 00:16:31.226
That one wall that my mother gave me
the freedom to draw on feels infinite.

00:16:31.250 --> 00:16:34.434
And it's for that reason
that I am still drawing.

00:16:34.458 --> 00:16:35.809
Thank you very much.

00:16:35.833 --> 00:16:41.542
(Cheers and applause)

